WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.520
A cell in the gut lining is not a neuron.

2
00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.280
How could it send a rapid message to a vagal sensory neuron?

3
00:00:10.783 --> 00:00:14.763
One possibility is a hormone released into nearby tissue.

4
00:00:16.043 --> 00:00:17.763
Another is a point-to-point synapse.

5
00:00:19.023 --> 00:00:20.823
Speed alone will not settle the choice.

6
00:00:21.867 --> 00:00:23.107
Start with anatomy.

7
00:00:25.027 --> 00:00:30.407
Modified rabies virus begins in selected enteroendocrine cells and labels connected

8
00:00:30.407 --> 00:00:35.427
nerves. In some mice, labelling reaches vagal nodose neurons.

9
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.540
Tracing can map a connection across the epithelial boundary.

10
00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:47.060
It cannot show rapid transmission, identify the messenger, or reveal whether the

11
00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:48.820
connection changes nerve firing.

12
00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:51.780
The picture must become physiology.

13
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.180
That establishes connectivity, not the transmitter.

14
00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:01.800
The cells also carry transcripts for VGLUT1.

15
00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:07.060
Those transcripts fit glutamate release, but still do not show a current.

16
00:01:08.183 --> 00:01:11.503
Now move the cell and a nodose neuron into a dish.

17
00:01:12.963 --> 00:01:14.283
Light activates the gut cell.

18
00:01:15.563 --> 00:01:20.683
Within 60 to 800 milliseconds, an excitatory current appears in the neuron.

19
00:01:21.783 --> 00:01:24.723
That current measures the neuron's response directly.

20
00:01:26.043 --> 00:01:29.703
Its sub-second latency supports a fast messenger in this dish.

21
00:01:30.863 --> 00:01:34.543
But one current still cannot establish which receptors carry it.

22
00:01:35.483 --> 00:01:40.863
Kynurenic acid blocks ionotropic glutamate receptors, and the current disappears.

23
00:01:42.203 --> 00:01:46.203
If receptors are blocked rather than cells damaged, what should happen after

24
00:01:46.203 --> 00:01:47.203
washout?

25
00:01:52.667 --> 00:01:55.947
Washout removes the blocker, and the current returns.

26
00:01:57.307 --> 00:02:00.707
Recovery supports receptor block rather than cell damage.

27
00:02:02.407 --> 00:02:07.707
Together, latency and reversible pharmacology identify fast glutamate action on the

28
00:02:07.707 --> 00:02:08.707
neuron.

29
00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:13.580
Here is the rule. No single result proves the route.

30
00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:20.080
A fast synapse is established when anatomy, timing, pharmacology and causal

31
00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:21.160
perturbation converge.

32
00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:24.510
Causality comes from the living mouse.

33
00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:29.030
Intestinal sucrose raises cervical-vagus firing.

34
00:02:30.410 --> 00:02:36.550
Blocking SGLT1 removes that response, placing the sugar sensor upstream.

35
00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:43.410
Light that silences CCK-labelled gut cells abolishes the sucrose response.

36
00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:49.250
Light that activates the same labelled population raises vagal firing.

37
00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:52.830
These tests ask necessity and sufficiency.

38
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:55.500
The chain has boundaries.

39
00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:58.060
Cocultures are dishes.

40
00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:04.520
Only a minority of CCK cells contacted sensory fibres in fixed tissue.

41
00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:07.980
A synapse does not erase hormone signalling.

42
00:03:09.133 --> 00:03:10.893
Anatomy found the contact.

43
00:03:12.293 --> 00:03:15.353
Physiology identified rapid glutamate transmission.

44
00:03:16.733 --> 00:03:20.373
Acute manipulations connected the sensor to vagal firing.

45
00:03:21.633 --> 00:03:24.033
The next question is what this signal changes.
